From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 11:29:15 +0200
Subject: net: dsa: lantiq: backport the GSWIP switch driver from net-next

v6.18 carries the GSWIP driver as a single monolithic file. net-next
has since split it into a regmap-only common core plus a thin
memory-mapped front end, in commit 322a1e6f3d68 ("net: dsa:
lantiq_gswip: split into common and MMIO parts"), and reshaped
struct gswip_hw_info around that split: the mii_ports bitmap and the
mii_port_reg_offset scalar were replaced by the per-port s16 mii_cfg[]
and mii_pcdu[] arrays with a -1 sentinel, and supports_2500m and
port_setup were added. New SoC variants are meant to be written against
that shape, so replace the v6.18 files with the net-next ones wholesale
rather than forward-porting a variant onto the older layout later.

Take the following byte for byte from net-next at c23807ef397c, where
drivers/net/dsa/lantiq is tree
c5b91afe67ba412d28200c883d986942553a042e:

  drivers/net/dsa/lantiq/lantiq_gswip_common.c  (new, 1727 lines)
  drivers/net/dsa/lantiq/lantiq_gswip.c
  drivers/net/dsa/lantiq/lantiq_gswip.h
  drivers/net/dsa/lantiq/lantiq_pce.h           (unchanged content)

tag_gswip.c is taken with one change, which the two cores make
necessary. It gains the FIELD_PREP(GSWIP_TX_PORT_MAP,
dsa_xmit_port_mask()) transmit port map, and its two receive-side early
exits do not free the socket buffer: dsa_switch_rcv() in this tree frees
the buffer itself when a tagger returns NULL, so a tagger that also
frees it double-frees. net-next's core leaves the buffer to the tagger
and its taggers free it, which is why the net-next file does. Neither
path is reachable while the switch delivers well formed frames from
ports that have network devices, so the fault would stay hidden until a
frame named a port with none - and the second free would then land on a
buffer already returned to the allocator and take a slab bug inside the
receive softirq.

Kconfig and Makefile are taken with one deliberate reduction: the
MaxLinear GSW1xx family is left out. Its driver is a serial-MDIO paging
scheme plus an SGMII SerDes driver, neither of which a memory-mapped SoC
block can reuse, and this tree has no such silicon to test it on. So
NET_DSA_LANTIQ_COMMON and its select from NET_DSA_LANTIQ_GSWIP are added
while NET_DSA_MXL_GSW1XX, mxl-gsw1xx*, tag_mxl-gsw1xx.c and
tag_mxl862xx.c are not carried. NET_DSA_LANTIQ_COMMON is the tristate
that builds the common core; without the select the switch driver links
against nothing.

MAINTAINERS needs no change: its entry already globs
drivers/net/dsa/lantiq/*.

No xRX500 compatible matches yet, so nothing in this tree probes the
driver.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

diff --git a/drivers/net/dsa/lantiq/Kconfig b/drivers/net/dsa/lantiq/Kconfig
index 1cb053c823f7..78db82a47d09 100644
--- a/drivers/net/dsa/lantiq/Kconfig
+++ b/drivers/net/dsa/lantiq/Kconfig
@@ -1,7 +1,12 @@
+config NET_DSA_LANTIQ_COMMON
+	tristate
+	select REGMAP
+
 config NET_DSA_LANTIQ_GSWIP
 	tristate "Lantiq / Intel GSWIP"
 	depends on HAS_IOMEM
 	select NET_DSA_TAG_GSWIP
+	select NET_DSA_LANTIQ_COMMON
 	help
 	  This enables support for the Lantiq / Intel GSWIP 2.1 found in
 	  the xrx200 / VR9 SoC.
